        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90011</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>The prognostic importance of distant lymph hub involvement in oligometastatic breast cancer is uncertain. <bold>Methods: </bold>We analyzed 98 patients with one to five metastatic lesions. Disease sites, local treatment, systemic therapy, and survival were recorded. Distant lymph hub disease was evaluated as a prespecified subgroup. <bold>Results: </bold>Patients with isolated distant lymph hub involvement had longer progression-free survival than those with visceral disease. Local treatment was more commonly used in the lymph hub subgroup. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Distant lymph hub involvement may define a clinically distinct oligometastatic presentation. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
